# The console's design system

Four pages (`/`, `/chat`, `/fleet`, `/node`) served by the same server, which
have to look like the same product. This document describes **the contract**
that keeps them that way — what lives where, and what is not allowed.

Code: [`src/webassets/`](../../src/webassets/) —
[`ui.py`](../../src/webassets/ui.py) (the system),
[`console.py`](../../src/webassets/console.py),
[`chat.py`](../../src/webassets/chat.py),
[`fleet.py`](../../src/webassets/fleet.py) (one page each), and
[`nodeview.py`](../../src/webassets/nodeview.py) (a *view* before it is a page:
the console, chat and fleet all mount it, and it also serves itself at `/node`).

## The rule

> `ui.py` is the **only** source of tokens, components and the app shell.
> A page adds nothing to it and redefines nothing from it.

```
STYLE_CSS = ui.CSS + nodeview.CSS + CONSOLE_PAGE_CSS   APP_JS   = ui.JS + nodeview.JS + CONSOLE_PAGE_JS
CHAT_CSS  = ui.CSS + nodeview.CSS + CHAT_PAGE_CSS      CHAT_JS  = ui.JS + nodeview.JS + CHAT_PAGE_JS
FLEET_CSS = ui.CSS + nodeview.CSS + FLEET_PAGE_CSS     FLEET_JS = ui.JS + nodeview.JS + FLEET_PAGE_JS
NODE_CSS  = ui.CSS + nodeview.CSS + NODE_PAGE_CSS      NODE_JS  = ui.JS + nodeview.JS + NODE_PAGE_JS
```

A token changed once changes every page. A page cannot quietly grow a second
button style: there is one within reach.

What is allowed in a page's own CSS: what exists only there — the graph and the
throughput canvas (console), the terminal and the host list (fleet), the
conversation bubbles (chat). If it starts to look reusable, it moves up into
`ui.py`. The only variables a page may declare are prefixed `--page-` (test:
`test_no_page_redefines_a_token`).

## Tokens, in three layers

One-way: **primitive → semantic → component**.

| Layer | Examples | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| primitive | `--n-050`, `--teal-400`, `--red-600` | nothing but the semantic layer |
| semantic | `--surface`, `--text-muted`, `--danger`, `--accent-soft` | the components |
| component | `--rail-w`, `--ctl-h`, `--btn-bg` | one specific component |

The light theme is declared on `:root` **with no media query**; dark is an
override, written twice from the same Python string (`_DARK_BODY`): once behind
`prefers-color-scheme` ("follow the system"), once behind `[data-theme="dark"]`
("I chose"). A colour defined **only** inside a media query does not exist when
the query does not match — hence the rule.

The explicit choice is set on `<html>` by `/theme.js`, loaded **blocking** in
`<head>`: otherwise the page paints the wrong theme for a frame. Since the CSP
forbids inline script, that tiny file cannot be a tag.

### Contrast is tested, not judged

[`tests/test_ui_contrast.py`](../../tests/test_ui_contrast.py) reads the tokens,
resolves the `var()` indirections, and measures the WCAG 2.1 ratio of every
text/background pair **in both themes**. Below 4.5 (text) or 3.0 (a field
border, large text), the test fails. A console that is hard to read at 3 a.m. is
a defect, not a preference.

## Components

Styled **on the element** where there is one (`button`, `input`, `table`,
`dialog`): a control that forgets its class still looks like the product.
Classes are for variants, not for the baseline.

`icon(name, title, extra)` takes an optional extra class, which is how a
disclosure chevron becomes `.ic.turn` without a second drawing.

`button` (+ `.primary` `.danger` `.ghost` `.sm` `.icon` `.wide`, `aria-busy` for
waiting) · `input`/`select`/`textarea` · `input[type=range]` + `.scale` · `.field`
(label + control + `.hint`) · `.check` (box and label in **one** target) ·
`.card` (`.card-head` / `.card-body` / `.card-foot`) · `details.card` · `.stat` /
`.stats` · `.badge` (tones `ok warn danger info accent`) · `.dot` · `table`
(sticky header, a `.table-wrap` that scrolls) · `.kv` · `.empty` / `errorHTML` /
`skeletonHTML` · `progress.meter` · `.notice` · `.toast` · `dialog` +
`.sheet-head/-body/-foot` · `.segmented` / `.subnav` / `.nav` · `.menu` ·
`.chips` · `.pager` · `.search` · `.kbd`.

Three states are **always** drawn, never left blank: *empty* (what to do so that
there is something), *loading* (a skeleton shaped like what is coming, so
nothing jumps), *error* (what failed).

## App shell (`SHELL`)

A rail on the left saying **where you are**, a top bar saying **what the node is
doing**, and one content column. `/chat` takes the rail's place for its
conversation list: the list **is** the navigation, and a second level of chrome
would be noise.

### Under 900 px: the rail becomes a bottom tab bar

Not a hamburger drawer. Navigation that costs two taps and hides where you are
is exactly what people complain about on a phone; a tab bar is always visible,
always says which section is open, and sits under the thumb. **It is the same
markup** — only the axis changes, so there is no second navigation to keep in
sync:

- the long label (`<span class="lbl">`) is hidden and `data-label` — the short
  name the palette already used — is picked up by a `::before`;
- the active tab carries a rule **and** the accent colour (never colour alone);
  the `.tail` counter becomes a badge;
- the brand and the rail's foot have no place in a 58 px strip: they move into
  the top bar's **⋯** menu (node state, links to the apps, search, sign out).
  That button is `display:none` above 900 px — declaring it **before** the media
  query is mandatory: at equal specificity the last rule wins, query or no;
- the content reserves `--tabbar-h` + `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` at the
  bottom, and the toasts rise by as much: nothing hides behind the bar.

### Menus (`.menu` + `MENU`)

Anything that would **grow a bar** lives in a dropdown: a button
`data-menu="id"` opens the panel with that id, one at a time, closed by Escape,
by a click elsewhere, or by its own scrim. Under 640 px the same markup becomes
a **sheet** anchored to the bottom of the screen, where the thumb already is. A
counter (`.count`) floats **over** its button, never beside it: a two-digit
number must not be able to widen the bar.

> **Trap.** A `backdrop-filter` makes its element the containing block for every
> `position:fixed` descendant. The top bar's blur therefore lives on a
> `::before`, or the sheet anchors under the bar instead of the bottom of the
> screen. Test: `test_the_topbar_does_not_capture_its_own_menus`.

### Dialogs

Above 640 px, a centred card. Below, a sheet rising from the bottom
(`inset:auto 0 0 0`), and a `dialog.full` takes the whole screen in `dvh` — not
`vh`, or the mobile browser's own bar crops the last row.

## Icons

One set, in `ui.py`: `ICONS` holds the paths, `icon(name, title)` writes the
wrapper. So every icon in the product shares a box, a stroke weight and a
baseline, and a page never inlines a glyph of its own.

- Painted with `currentColor` and sized in `em`, so an icon is the colour and
  the size of the text beside it, in either theme, without a second rule.
- `aria-hidden` by default — the button next to it already has a label. Pass a
  `title` only when the icon is alone and *is* the label.
- `.ic.turn` is a chevron rotated by state (`[aria-expanded="true"]`), so a
  disclosure arrow is one drawing turned rather than two kept in step.

**Never an emoji.** They render as a different picture on every platform, say
nothing to a screen reader, and make a product look cheap. The one exception is
content that is genuinely an emoji — chat's reaction palette, and what somebody
types. `test_no_emoji_in_the_interface` walks every page's markup, stylesheet
and script.

The same goes outside the browser: terminal output uses aligned words
(`[ ok ]`, `[warn]`, `[fail]`), because a dingbat is not guaranteed to render in
a minimal console and a word always is; documentation uses a heading or a table
where it would have used a tick.

## Shared runtime (`ui.JS`)

What each of the pages used to solve on its own:

- `$`, `$$`, `esc`, `shortId`, `debounce`;
- `api` / `apiJson` — the token in `sessionStorage`, a 401 sends you back to the
  gate;
- formatting: `fmtBytes`, `fmtRate`, `fmtDuration`, `fmtAgo`, `fmtNum`;
- feedback: `toast` (polite, `aria-live`), `setMessage` (next to the control),
  `withBusy` (the label stays put during the request);
- `setHTML` — writes only when the markup actually changed, so a list painted on
  a timer does not replace a node under the pointer and swallow the click;
- `confirmAction` — destructive actions ask, in the page;
- `THEME` — light / dark / system, remembered per browser;
- `EVENTS` — the node's change stream (`/api/events`): what moved, coalesced into
  at most ten repaints a second, one handler call per frame. Structure only —
  the numbers that never stop moving stay on `REFRESH`'s interval;
- `REFRESH` — that interval, and the **list** of views on it (`REFRESH.on`,
  `REFRESH.mount`). A list rather than one job, so a view that shows a moving
  number registers itself instead of waiting to be remembered;
- `CONTEXT` — **which node this page is driving**: the header every call carries,
  the epoch that makes a switch atomic, the subscription every view resets on,
  and `CTX_BAR`, the strip that says so on all three pages;
- `OPEN` / `openLinked` — how a link into another app opens (a window, a tab, or
  by screen size), also per browser;
- `ROUTER` — `#section/subsection`: linkable, bookmarkable, **Back** works;
- `PALETTE` — `⌘K` / `Ctrl-K`, every section and the common actions;
- `MENU` — the shell's dropdowns; `MENU.onShow[id]` lets a page paint its
  contents when it is looked at, not on every poll;
- `mountShell()` — wires all of that once per page.

## Constraints you cannot see

- **No external resource.** No CDN, no web font, no remote image. The console
  works on a machine with no internet.
- **No `style=` attribute.** The CSP is `default-src 'self'` without
  `unsafe-inline`: a `style="width:42%"` is ignored **silently** by the browser.
  A bar written that way never fills and nobody sees an error — which is exactly
  what used to happen. Variable values go through a
  `<progress class="meter" value max>` or a CSSOM assignment
  (`element.style.width = …`, which the CSP does not cover). Test:
  `test_no_inline_style_attribute_anywhere`.
- **Everything is escaped.** Anything from the network (ids, labels, SSH
  banners, command output) goes through `esc()` before entering `innerHTML`.
- **Nothing is frameable.** Every page keeps `frame-ancestors 'none'`. Showing a
  node inside chat is a *mount*, not a frame: the same view, in the same
  document.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` respected; a visible focus ring everywhere; a **Skip
  to content** link at the head of every page; targets ≥ 24 px; fields at 16 px
  on mobile (or iOS zooms); `tabular-nums` on numbers people compare.
- **Colour is never the only signal**: every status badge carries its own word
  (`managed`, `no key`, `waiting on you`).
- **No layout may take its minimum from its content.** Every `fr` grid track is
  written `minmax(0,1fr)`, never a bare `1fr`. A bare `1fr` is
  `minmax(auto,1fr)`, and that `auto` minimum is the content's *min-content*
  width — so one pasted URL, which is one very long word, makes the track wider
  than the screen and the whole page scrolls sideways. That is not theoretical:
  it is what a long chat message did on a phone. Test:
  `test_no_grid_track_takes_its_minimum_from_its_content`.
- **A percentage `max-width` needs a parent with a width.** Capping a bubble at
  `88%` of a row that was itself shrink-to-fit is circular, and a browser
  settles it by undersizing. Percentage caps hang off a box whose width is
  already decided.
- **A flex child shrinks unless told not to.** In a scrolling column — a message
  log, a list — the children must be `flex:none`, or once they are taller than
  the pane the browser shrinks *them* instead of scrolling: content crushed and
  overlapping, no scrollbar. Test:
  `test_the_chat_log_cannot_squash_its_messages`.

## Adding something

- **A section** in a page: a `<button role="tab" data-tab="x"
  data-label="Short"><span class="lbl">The long label</span></button>` in the
  rail, a `<section data-panel="x">`, and `ROUTER` does the rest (URL, title,
  Back, focus). Both labels are mandatory: the short one is what the tab bar and
  the palette use. A subsection: `data-subtab` / `data-sub`.
- **A menu**: a `data-menu="x"` button and a `<div id="x" class="menu" hidden>`.
  The `hidden` attribute is not optional either — without it the panel shows on
  load and intercepts the click of the button meant to open it.
- **A component**: in `ui.py`, with the semantic tokens — never a hard-coded
  colour. If it carries text, add the pair to the contrast test.
- **A page**: a module exporting `X_HTML`, `X_PAGE_CSS`, `X_PAGE_JS`, assembled
  in `__init__.py`. Reuse the shell, the confirmation `<dialog>`, the toast
  region and the palette: `test_every_element_the_scripts_reach_for_exists`
  fails if one is missing.
